        "status": "accepted",
        "notes": "review1 doubtful: Slovenian, English, accept and examples are fine. The doubt is the helper. grlo comes from Proto-Slavic *gъrdlo, PIE *gʷerh₃-, and the lookup names Latin gurges among its cognates, so German Gurgel can only be a distant link through Latin, which the Gurgel page does not confirm. English gargle is traced by Wiktionary to French gargouiller and is not shown as related, so offering it as a comparison looks like forced resemblance. Keep Gurgel only if a native or etymology source confirms it, otherwise set helper to null.; review2 doubtful: Slovenian, gloss, examples and accept are fine. The doubt is the helper: Gurgel is a real but remote relative (PIE *gwerh3- through Latin gurgulio), while the lookup traces gargle only to Old French gargouille and shows no link to grlo, so that half may be forced resemblance. Fix: grlo, throat: distantly related to German Gurgel."
      }
